.Dd August 1, 2026
.Dt T150D 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm t150d
.Nd force feedback daemon for the Thrustmaster T150
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl e Ar endpoint
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the macOS half of the force feedback bridge. It listens on loopback for
the proxy
.Pa dinput8.dll
running inside a CrossOver bottle, turns the effects that arrive into the
wheel's own packets, and hands them to a backend.
.Pp
It needs no entitlement, no root and no system extension. It must run as the
console user, because
.Fn IOHIDDeviceSetReport
is gated on that privilege, and it must not be sandboxed, because App Sandbox
breaks IOKit HID access.
.Pp
One client is served at a time. A second connection may displace the first,
because the usual reason for one is a game that crashed and was restarted,
but only after it has proved the token and only after the first has been put
back into a safe state. A newcomer that does not authenticate within two
seconds, or that sends anything other than a token, is dropped without the
current client noticing: anything on the machine can reach loopback, and
displacing on the connection alone would let any local process end a game's
force feedback at will.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl g Ar ms
Pause this long after every packet sent to the wheel. Off by default. The
probe tools have always left 20 milliseconds between writes, on a comment
saying the wheel drops back to back packets, but nothing has ever tested
whether that is needed and the interrupt OUT route works with none. A floor
on the write rate would cap effect updates well below what force feedback
wants, so this exists to put the pause back if a real wheel asks for it.
.It Fl n
Drive nothing. Print every packet that would have gone to the wheel instead,
in the form
.Xr t150-probe 1
prints. This is the only behaviour on anything but macOS.
.It Fl e Ar endpoint
Where to publish the port and token. The default is
.Pa ~/Library/Application Support/t150ffb/endpoint ,
which the proxy reads through Wine's
.Pa Z:
mapping.
.It Fl v
Log connections, effect downgrades and every entry into the safe state.
.El
.Sh THE ENDPOINT FILE
.Nm
binds an ephemeral port and writes two lines, the port and a per-run token:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
49713
6a607d5fcea96d0e8c3356cc56790fe9
.Ed
.Pp
It is written to a temporary and renamed, so a client never reads half a
file, and created with mode 0600. The token stops another local process
driving the motors by guessing a port. It is not a security boundary against
a process running as the same user, and it is not meant to be: what bounds
the damage is the watchdog.
.Sh THE WATCHDOG
Nothing in this stack learns that a game has exited. Wine's
.Pa hidclass.sys
consumes the close at the physical device object, and DirectInput sends a
reset only on a graceful unacquire, so a game that crashes or is force quit
leaves the wheel holding whatever force it was last given.
.Pp
.Nm
therefore treats silence as a fault. If no frame arrives for half a second it
stops every effect and releases the autocenter, whether or not the socket is
still open, which leaves the wheel limp rather than latched. A client is
expected to send
.Dv T150_OP_KEEPALIVE
often enough to stay ahead of this. The same safe state is entered when a
client disconnects, when one displaces another, and on the way out.
.Sh EFFECTS
The wheel renders constant force, sine, both sawtooths, spring and damper.
Square and triangle are not in its protocol, and neither are friction,
inertia or ramp.
.Nm
downgrades those rather than refusing them, because a game that gets
.Dv DIERR_UNSUPPORTED
from
.Fn CreateEffect
may switch force feedback off altogether: square and triangle become sine,
friction and inertia become damper, and a ramp becomes a constant that
.Nm
re-sends as it slides.
.Pp
A per-effect gain has no equivalent on the wheel, which has one device gain,
so it is folded into the effect's magnitude before encoding.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/Library/Application Support/t150ffb/endpoint
The port and token, removed on exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr crossover-wheel 7 ,
.Xr t150-probe 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Renaud Allard
.Sh CAVEATS
The wheel honours settings and force feedback alike through an unprivileged
.Fn IOHIDDeviceSetReport
with the device left to macOS, which is the transport this daemon uses. Both
were measured; the procedure is in
.Pa docs/PROBES.md .
.Pp
What has not been measured is this daemon driving a wheel end to end. The
backend is written and compiles against the real frameworks, and every packet
it sends is one a probe tool has already moved the wheel with, but no game
has yet reached a wheel through it.
.Pp
Only a constant force and one periodic have ever been played. Springs,
dampers, envelopes, ramps and per-effect gain are arithmetic derived from
a Linux driver rather than measured, so expect to find at least one of them
wrong.
